## Architecture

For notes: **screen → controller → repository contract → storage adapter**. Dependencies are supplied
by providers. Use `ref.watch` for reactive dependencies, `ref.read` for actions, and `ref.onDispose` for
owned resources. Dispose text controllers, router instances, streams and clients.

Models are immutable; never mutate a list obtained from a provider. Domain validation lives in models
or application services. A controller publishes a new state only after a successful write, prevents
concurrent writes, and checks `ref.mounted` after awaits. Keep simple ephemeral input and busy state
in the screen. SettingsController is the small adapter for appearance preferences.

Screens render loading, empty, data and error states, forward actions, and show understandable errors.
They do not access storage or HTTP directly. Reusable presentational widgets accept props/callbacks.
Never erase corrupt data automatically: surface the failure and offer retry/recovery explicitly.
Do not expose raw exception details in UI messages. Initial loading retries are explicit rather than
Riverpod's automatic retry, so broken stored data does not cause a retry loop.

## UI editing contract

- Start with `lib/ui/tokens.dart` and `lib/app/theme.dart`; features use semantic colors, not new palettes.
- `WorkspaceShell` and `AppSurface` accept presentation props only. Providers and routes belong to screens.
{% if choices.ui == 'glass' -%}
- The design is Liquid Glass (`useGlass = true` in `lib/ui/design.dart`). It uses Flutter backdrop blur,
  not platform-specific SwiftUI or a shader. Keep filters clipped and out of scrolling collection items.
- Preserve opaque fallbacks for high contrast/accessibility, 48-pixel controls, readable text, focus,
  keyboard scrolling, selectable notes, sidebar/compact navigation and system/light/dark appearance.
{% else -%}
- The design is Material 3 with opaque surfaces (`useGlass = false` in `lib/ui/design.dart`).
- Preserve 48-pixel controls, readable text, focus, keyboard scrolling, selectable notes,
  sidebar/compact navigation and system/light/dark appearance.
{% endif -%}
- Layout uses Flutter constraints and supports 320-pixel phones, keyboards, wide windows and large text.
{% if choices.styling == 'tailwind' -%}
- Styling is Mix 2.2.1 / mix_winds 0.1.0-alpha.1 (experimental). `styled_panel.dart` uses
  `p-6 rounded-3xl border border-line bg-panel` with explicit `panel`/`line` aliases in `TwConfig`.
- Utilities render native widgets, not CSS. Do not add Tailwind's CLI, npm, web-only classes or a WebView.
  Unsupported utilities throw via `onDiagnostic`; never disable that check to hide a token error.
- Read [mix_winds](https://pub.dev/packages/mix_winds) and its Flutter constraint adaptations before
  expanding utilities. Prefer native layout/input widgets around styled panels. There is no generation
  step. Preserve exact package pins, theme-driven aliases and tests when changing the styling adapter.
{% else -%}
- Styling is standard Flutter. `styled_panel.dart` defines spacing, fill, border and radius with typed
  widgets. Extend the small presentation kit when reused; do not invent a CSS parser or widget DSL.
{% endif %}
## Commands and configuration

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `make setup` | `flutter pub get --enforce-lockfile`, then configure |
| `make dev` | Preview at http://127.0.0.1:{{ ports.WEB_PORT }} |
| `make check` | Formatting + strict analysis + all unit/widget tests |
| `make test` | Tests with app defines |
| `make format` | Dart formatting |
| `make build` / `make start` | Release web build / local server |
| `make devices` | Device IDs |
| `dart run tool/project.dart run -d <id>` | Device/Chrome development and terminal hot reload |
| `make android` / `make ios` | Local APK / unsigned iOS release |
| `dart run tool/project.dart test --coverage` | Test coverage |

Use these entry points, not raw `flutter run/test/build`: they inject `.env` configuration. Flutter's
Dart SDK must be on the login PATH. fastDev commands have closed stdin; stop/restart Dev after edits.
Dev answers HTTP before compilation ends: open the page after it prints `is being served at`.
Native commands need their platform SDKs (`flutter doctor -v`). A browser check does not prove a native
permission, plugin, signing or lifecycle change works; run the relevant native build/device check and
report exactly what was exercised.

`.env` is private local configuration; `.env.example` is tracked. `APP_NAME` is the display name;
`APP_IDENTIFIER` is the Android/iOS ID; `APP_SLUG` is a stable storage namespace; `WEB_PORT` is allocated
by fastDev. Keep the Dart package `app` stable. Do not manually edit the generated public JSON or
Android app name/ID files. Native Info.plist, bundle identifiers and web metadata are refreshed by
the configuration tool. Keep other native settings intact.

To add a configuration key: document it in `.env.example` and README, validate it in `tool/config.dart`,
add it to `publicDefines` **only if it is public**, and add a typed field in AppConfig and tests.
Client applications cannot keep secrets in compiled defines or assets. No keys from `.env` are forwarded
implicitly, and shell-like expressions in names remain literal.

## Add a feature or screen

1. Name `lib/features/<feature>/`; define its immutable data and repository interface.
2. Implement storage/network I/O under `data/`; register its dependency provider. Prefer fakeable interfaces.
3. Add an `AsyncNotifier` under `application/`, with validation, explicit failures, and safe writes.
4. Build the screen under `presentation/`; wire its route in `lib/app/router.dart` and pass callbacks to reusable UI.
5. Write unit tests of business rules and failures, repository tests, and widget tests of user acceptance criteria.
6. Check narrow screens, keyboard, scrolling, accessibility semantics, light/dark modes and navigation/back behavior.
7. Update the product specification and docs, then run the quality gate and release build.

For an API: add a client adapter in `core/network/` only when a real endpoint exists; inject it into a
feature repository. Model timeouts, malformed responses, authorization, retries and cancellation;
never issue HTTP from a widget. Keep server code in a separate service when needed.
For important persistent data: select a transactional database, introduce versioned migrations in that
adapter, test upgrades from the previous schema and handle recovery before replacing demo preferences.
Add a separate use-case layer only when logic is reused or too complex for a controller.

## Tests and definition of done

Place tests in `test/*_test.dart`; share fakes under `test/support/`. Use `ProviderContainer.test`
with provider overrides for controllers. Use the official in-memory SharedPreferencesAsync platform
adapter for storage tests; no device or credentials are needed. For widgets, pump a ProviderScope and
exercise complete user interactions. Simulate load/write failures and delayed writes, not only success.
Do not suppress analyzer warnings or reformat unrelated native files to make a check pass.

Before finishing: `make check` passes with zero analyzer warnings/infos; `make build` succeeds;
acceptance criteria, docs and tests match the result. Also exercise the relevant native target for native
changes. Dependency changes include a regenerated lockfile (`flutter pub get`, then `make setup` and
checks), and an explanation. Keep `.env`, generated files, dependencies and build outputs out of Git.
Everything—code, comments, UI strings, documentation and commit messages—is English. Use Dart's
formatter, snake_case filenames, PascalCase types, typed values and descriptive component names.
